// Blog posts batch 2 (2026-10-16): butcher's steaks, stir-fries and grilling. Rules in posts-blog.js.
import { BLOG_KEYWORDS } from './posts-blog-keywords.js'

const meta = (slug) => ({ slug, kw: BLOG_KEYWORDS[slug].focus, keywords: BLOG_KEYWORDS[slug].keywords, date: '2026-10-16', updated: '2026-10-16' })

export const BLOG_POSTS_2 = [
  {
    ...meta('beef-onglet-steak'),
    title: 'Beef Onglet Steak: The Butcher’s Cut and How to Cook It',
    seoTitle: 'Beef Onglet Steak: Expert Cooking Guide',
    excerpt: `Beef onglet steak (hanger steak) explained: where it comes from, how to trim, sear and slice it, and how it compares with fillet, rump and flank.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Onglet is the steak butchers used to keep for themselves. There is only one per animal, it does not look like much on the counter, and for years most people had never heard of it. Then chefs discovered what butchers already knew: cooked hot and fast and sliced properly, a beef onglet steak is tender, juicy and has one of the deepest, richest flavours of any cut. In English it is usually called hanger steak, because it hangs from the diaphragm between the ribs and the loin.`],
      ['p', `This guide explains what onglet is, how to prepare and cook it, and why it is one of the best-value steaks you can buy.`],
      ['h2', `What is a beef onglet steak?`],
      ['p', `The onglet is a thick, V-shaped muscle that supports the diaphragm. Because it does little work, it is naturally tender, and because it sits close to the kidneys and is well supplied with blood, it has a strong, almost mineral beef flavour that some people compare to liver in its richness, without any of the offal taste. A single hanger weighs around 500 g to 1 kg and is made up of two lobes joined by a tough central sinew.`],
      ['p', `In France it is a bistro classic, served as onglet à l'échalote with shallots and frites. In the United States, hanger steak became a restaurant favourite in the 1990s, and Australian chefs have followed. Our [beef hanger steak](/product/beef-hanger-steak/) is the same cut.`],
      ['h2', `How to prepare onglet`],
      ['p', `Most onglets need a little trimming. Lay the steak flat and you will see a line of thick, silvery connective tissue running down the centre. Run a sharp knife along each side of that sinew to separate the two lobes, giving you two long, even steaks. Remove any remaining silverskin from the surface. Pat the steaks dry and season with salt at least 40 minutes before cooking, or just before cooking; avoid salting 5 to 10 minutes ahead, as that draws moisture to the surface without time for it to reabsorb.`],
      ['h2', `How to cook a beef steak like onglet`],
      ['p', `If you are learning how to cook a beef steak, onglet is a good teacher, because it punishes overcooking and rewards confidence. The method is simple:`],
      ['ul', [
        `Bring the steak out of the fridge 30 minutes before cooking.`,
        `Heat a heavy pan or grill until it is very hot and lightly smoking.`,
        `Add a thin film of high-smoke-point oil and lay the steak away from you.`,
        `Cook for 3 to 4 minutes a side, turning once or twice, until well browned.`,
        `In the last minute, add a knob of butter, a crushed garlic clove and thyme, and baste.`,
        `Rest for 6 to 8 minutes on a warm plate before slicing.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Onglet is best served rare to medium-rare: aim for 50 to 54°C in the centre before resting. Past medium, its loose fibres tighten and it becomes chewy. Slice it across the grain, which on onglet runs at an angle, so check each piece before you cut.`],
      ['h3', `Blue beef steak and onglet`],
      ['p', `A blue beef steak is seared briefly on the outside and left almost raw inside, at around 45 to 50°C. Onglet is one of the few cuts that suits blue or very rare cooking, because its flavour is strong and its texture stays tender. If you prefer this style, use a very hot pan, cook for about 90 seconds a side, and rest briefly. Always buy steak for very rare cooking from a supplier you trust and keep it properly chilled until cooking.`],
      ['h2', `Onglet vs beef fillet, rump and flank`],
      ['p', `How does onglet compare with the cuts you already know?`],
      ['ul', [
        `Beef fillet (eye fillet): more tender and very mild. Onglet has far more flavour and costs much less. Our [beef eye fillet steaks](/product/beef-eye-fillet-steaks/) remain the choice for the most tender, delicate steak.`,
        `[Rump](/product/rump-beef-steaks/): firmer, beefy and good value, more forgiving at medium.`,
        `[Flank](/product/flank-steak-beef/): leaner and coarser, ideal for marinating and slicing for salads.`,
        `[Chuck tail flap](/product/beef-chuck-tail-flap/): similar loose texture with more marbling.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Best cut for slow cooked beef? Not onglet`],
      ['p', `Onglet is a fast-cooking steak. If you want the best cut for slow cooked beef, choose shin, cheek or chuck instead: their connective tissue melts during a long braise and they become spoon-tender. Onglet has very little of that collagen, so braising it simply dries it out. Our guides to [braised beef](/blog/braised-beef/) and [slow cooked beef cheeks](/blog/slow-cooked-beef-cheeks/) cover the right cuts for that job.`],
      ['h2', `Serving ideas`],
      ['ul', [
        `Classic French: with a quick shallot sauce and chips.`,
        `With chimichurri, grilled corn and a tomato salad.`,
        `Sliced over rice with a soy, ginger and spring onion dressing.`,
        `In a steak sandwich with caramelised onions and mustard.`,
        `Over a peppery rocket and parmesan salad.`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `A simple shallot sauce`],
      ['p', `After cooking, pour off most of the fat from the pan. Add two finely sliced shallots and a pinch of salt and cook gently for 3 minutes. Add 150 ml of beef stock and a teaspoon of red or white vinegar, scrape up the browned bits, and simmer until reduced by half. Stir in a knob of butter and some chopped parsley, and spoon over the sliced steak.`],
      ['h2', `Beef onglet steak questions`],
      ['h3', `Why is onglet so cheap?`],
      ['p', `There is only one per animal, it has an awkward shape and a central sinew to remove, and until recently few people knew what to do with it. That keeps the price lower than the famous premium steaks, even though its flavour rivals any of them.`],
      ['h3', `Can I marinate onglet?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Its loose texture takes marinades well. Soy and garlic, lime and chilli, or olive oil with rosemary all suit it. Two to six hours is plenty.`],
      ['h3', `How many people does one hanger feed?`],
      ['p', `Once trimmed, a 700 g hanger yields about 550 g of steak, enough for two hearty serves or three lighter ones with sides.`],
      ['h3', `What wood or fuel suits onglet on the barbecue?`],
      ['p', `Lump charcoal gives the hottest, cleanest heat and the best crust. If you use gas, preheat with the lid closed for 15 minutes so the grill bars are as hot as possible, and keep the lid open while the steak cooks so it sears rather than bakes.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal onglet and fillet online`],
      ['p', `Our [hanger steak](/product/beef-hanger-steak/) and premium [eye fillet](/product/beef-eye-fillet-steaks/) are both certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia. Browse the full [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/) to compare steaks and prices per kilo, and read our [beef bavette](/blog/beef-bavette/) guide for another under-rated butcher's cut.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-bavette'),
    title: 'Beef Bavette: What the Cut Is and How to Cook It Well',
    seoTitle: 'Beef Bavette Guide: Tender, Easy Steak',
    excerpt: `Beef bavette explained: which muscle it is, how it compares with flank and skirt, how to cook and slice it, and the closest halal cuts we deliver.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Bavette is a French butchery term that has become fashionable on Australian restaurant menus, and for good reason. A beef bavette steak is loose-grained, beefy and full of juice, and it costs a fraction of the premium steaks. The catch is that "bavette" means slightly different cuts to different butchers, and it needs the right cooking and slicing to be tender. This guide clears up the confusion and shows you how to get the best from it.`],
      ['h2', `What is beef bavette?`],
      ['p', `In French, bavette means "bib", a reference to its flat, apron-like shape. In France, bavette d'aloyau is the flap of meat from the bottom of the sirloin, while bavette de flanchet is from the flank. In English-speaking countries, the name is used for either the flank steak or the sirloin flap (also called flap meat), and occasionally for skirt. All three are flat, coarse-grained muscles from the underside of the animal, and they cook in very similar ways.`],
      ['p', `What they share is a pronounced grain that runs along the length of the cut, a good amount of flavour, and a texture that is tender when cooked medium-rare and sliced thinly across the grain.`],
      ['h2', `Bavette cut beef vs flank and skirt`],
      ['ul', [
        `Sirloin flap bavette: well marbled and loose, with a rich flavour and the most forgiving texture.`,
        `[Flank steak](/product/flank-steak-beef/): leaner, thicker and more uniform, with very obvious long fibres. Ideal for marinating.`,
        `Skirt: thinner and looser than both, with the most intense flavour. See our [beef skirt](/blog/beef-skirt/) guide.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `When a recipe calls for bavette cut beef, any of these will work. In our range, [flank steak](/product/flank-steak-beef/) is the closest match for the French bavette de flanchet, and [chuck tail flap](/product/beef-chuck-tail-flap/) behaves very much like the well-marbled sirloin flap: loose, juicy and great on the barbecue.`],
      ['h2', `Wagyu beef bavette`],
      ['p', `Wagyu beef bavette is prized by chefs because Wagyu's marbling makes an already juicy cut even richer. It is not something we list at the moment, but if you want the Wagyu experience in a fast-cooking steak, our [Wagyu rump](/product/wagyu-beef-rump-steak-mb5/) and [Wagyu oyster blade](/product/wagyu-oyster-blade-steaks-mb9/) steaks give a similar, deeply marbled result. See our [Wagyu steak](/wagyu-steak/) page for the full range and marble scores.`],
      ['h2', `How to cook bavette`],
      ['p', `Bavette loves high heat and a short cooking time:`],
      ['ul', [
        `Season generously with salt and pepper, or marinate for 1 to 6 hours.`,
        `Heat a grill, barbecue or heavy pan until very hot.`,
        `Cook for 3 to 5 minutes a side, depending on thickness, to medium-rare (52 to 55°C before resting).`,
        `Rest for 8 minutes, loosely covered with foil.`,
        `Slice thinly across the grain at a slight angle.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Do not cook bavette past medium. Its long fibres contract as they heat, so well-done bavette is chewy no matter how well you slice it.`],
      ['h3', `Marinades for bavette`],
      ['p', `Bavette absorbs flavour quickly. Try olive oil, garlic, rosemary and lemon zest for a classic French bistro steak; soy, honey, garlic and ginger for an Asian-style grill; or lime, cumin, chilli and coriander for tacos. Avoid leaving it in very acidic marinades overnight.`],
      ['h2', `Beef slices for sandwiches, salads and stir-fries`],
      ['p', `Because it slices so neatly, bavette is perfect for beef slices in steak sandwiches, grain bowls and salads. For stir-fries, slice raw bavette very thinly across the grain (partially freezing it for 20 minutes makes this easier) and cook it in batches over the highest heat. Or skip the knife work with our ready-cut [beef stir-fry strips](/product/beef-stir-fry-strips/). For a cold salad, our [Thai beef salad](/blog/thai-beef-salad/) recipe works perfectly with bavette.`],
      ['h2', `Bavette steak frites at home`],
      ['p', `For the bistro classic, cook the bavette as above, and while it rests, make a quick sauce in the same pan: sizzle a sliced shallot in butter, add 150 ml of beef stock and a teaspoon of Dijon mustard, reduce by half and finish with chopped parsley. Serve the sliced steak with oven chips or crisp pan-fried potatoes and a green salad.`],
      ['h2', `What about minced beef from these cuts?`],
      ['p', `Flank and flap are lean, flavourful muscles, which is why butchers often send their trimmings to the mincer. If you want minced beef for burgers, sauces and meatballs, our [premium beef mince](/product/premium-beef-mince/) and [extra lean beef mince](/product/extra-lean-beef-mince/) are ready to cook, and our [beef meat patties recipe](/blog/beef-meat-patties-recipe/) shows how to shape the perfect burger.`],
      ['h2', `Beef bavette questions`],
      ['h3', `Is bavette the same as flank?`],
      ['p', `Sometimes. In France, bavette can mean either the flank or the sirloin flap. In Australia, a bavette on a menu is usually one of those two cuts. They cook the same way.`],
      ['h3', `Is bavette a cheap steak?`],
      ['p', `It is usually much cheaper per kilo than scotch fillet or eye fillet, which makes it one of the best-value steaks for flavour.`],
      ['h3', `How thick should bavette be?`],
      ['p', `Most bavette is 2 to 3 cm thick. Thinner pieces cook in 2 minutes a side; thicker ones need 4 to 5 minutes a side.`],
      ['h2', `Resting and carving bavette`],
      ['p', `Resting is not optional with thin, loose-grained steaks. As the steak cooks, the outer layers tighten and push juices towards the centre. Given 8 minutes on a warm plate, the fibres relax and the juices redistribute, so far less ends up on the board when you slice. Tent the steak loosely with foil; wrapping it tightly steams the crust and softens it.`],
      ['p', `To carve, first look at the surface of the cooked steak and find the direction of the fibres. With bavette they run along the length of the cut, so cut the steak in half crossways first, then turn each piece 90 degrees and slice thinly, holding the knife at a 45-degree angle. The angled cut gives wider, more tender slices. Pour any resting juices back over the meat before serving.`],
      ['h2', `Bavette for a crowd`],
      ['p', `Because bavette cooks so fast, it is ideal for feeding a group without spending hours at the grill. Cook several pieces at once on a large barbecue, rest them together on a tray, and slice them onto a platter just before serving. Set out bowls of chimichurri, garlic sauce, salsa verde and pickled onions, plus warm flatbreads, and let everyone help themselves. Allow about 180 g of raw meat per person for a main course, or 120 g if there are plenty of sides.`],
      ['p', `Leftover slices are excellent cold the next day in a baguette with mustard and rocket, tossed through a grain salad, or quickly reheated in a hot pan for fried rice. Keep them covered in the fridge and use them within two days.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal beef online`],
      ['p', `Flank, chuck tail flap, Wagyu steaks and beef mince are all in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, Australia-wide. Compare prices per kilo online, and read our [beef onglet steak](/blog/beef-onglet-steak/) guide for another chef's favourite.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-schnitzel'),
    title: 'Beef Schnitzel: Crisp, Tender Recipes and the Right Cut',
    seoTitle: 'Easy Beef Schnitzel Recipes and Meals',
    excerpt: `How to make beef schnitzel that is crisp outside and tender inside: the best cut, pounding and crumbing, frying or air frying, and easy schnitzel meals.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `A golden beef schnitzel is pub-dinner comfort at home: thin, tender beef in a crunchy crumb, served with chips and salad, or turned into a parmigiana with tomato sauce and melted cheese. It is also one of the most economical ways to feed a family, because pounding the meat thin makes a small amount go a long way and tenderises it at the same time. This guide covers the cut, the crumbing technique, cooking methods and some favourite beef schnitzel meals.`],
      ['h2', `Which cut makes the best beef schnitzel?`],
      ['p', `Schnitzel needs a lean cut with no big seams of fat or gristle, because the meat cooks in a few minutes and has no time to soften connective tissue. The best options are:`],
      ['ul', [
        `Topside: the traditional Australian schnitzel cut. Lean, even and economical. Buy a [whole topside](/product/beef-topside-whole/) and slice your own.`,
        `[Minute steaks](/product/beef-minute-steaks/): already thin and ready to crumb.`,
        `[Rump](/product/rump-beef-steaks/): more flavour and slightly more tender than topside.`,
        `[Porterhouse minute steaks](/product/beef-porterhouse-minute-steaks/): a premium, tender schnitzel.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Avoid chuck, blade and shin for schnitzel. They are full of connective tissue that needs long cooking, so they turn chewy when fried quickly.`],
      ['h2', `Pounding the beef`],
      ['p', `Slice the beef across the grain into steaks about 1 cm thick. Place each between two sheets of baking paper or inside a large zip-lock bag and pound with a meat mallet or the bottom of a heavy pan, working from the centre outwards, until it is about 4 to 5 mm thick. Pounding breaks up the muscle fibres and makes an even thickness, so the schnitzel cooks quickly and evenly.`],
      ['h2', `The three-step crumb`],
      ['p', `Set up three shallow dishes: seasoned plain flour, beaten egg (with a splash of milk), and breadcrumbs. Panko gives the crunchiest result; fine dry crumbs give a classic, even crust. Dredge each piece of beef in flour and shake off the excess, dip it in egg, then press it firmly into the crumbs on both sides. For an extra thick crust, repeat the egg and crumb steps. Rest the crumbed schnitzels in the fridge for 15 to 30 minutes so the coating sets.`],
      ['h3', `Flavouring the crumb`],
      ['p', `Add finely grated parmesan, dried oregano, garlic powder, lemon zest, chopped parsley or smoked paprika to the breadcrumbs. Two tablespoons of parmesan per cup of crumbs gives a beautifully savoury crust.`],
      ['h2', `Beef schnitzel recipes: frying, baking and air frying`],
      ['p', `Most beef schnitzel recipes use one of three methods:`],
      ['ul', [
        `Shallow frying: heat 1 cm of oil in a heavy frying pan to about 175°C. Cook for 2 to 3 minutes a side until deep golden. Drain on a rack, not paper towel, to keep the bottom crisp.`,
        `Oven baking: spray both sides with oil and bake on a rack over a tray at 220°C for 12 to 15 minutes, turning once.`,
        `Air frying: spray with oil and cook at 200°C for 8 to 10 minutes, turning halfway. Do not overcrowd the basket.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Because the beef is thin, it is cooked through by the time the crumb is golden. Season with salt as soon as it comes out of the pan.`],
      ['h2', `Beef schnitzel meals`],
      ['ul', [
        `Classic: with chips, salad and a wedge of lemon.`,
        `Parmigiana: top with tomato sauce and mozzarella and grill until bubbling.`,
        `Holstein: topped with a fried egg.`,
        `Schnitzel burger: in a toasted bun with lettuce, tomato and mayonnaise.`,
        `Schnitzel wrap: sliced with tabbouleh, garlic sauce and pickles.`,
        `With mashed potato, peas and a mushroom or pepper sauce.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Cooking for a crowd`],
      ['p', `Crumb the schnitzels in the morning and keep them on a tray in the fridge, separated by baking paper. Fry them in batches just before serving and keep the cooked ones warm on a rack in a 100°C oven. Crumbed, uncooked schnitzels also freeze well for up to two months; cook them straight from frozen in the oven or air fryer, adding a few minutes.`],
      ['h2', `Best piece of beef to roast instead?`],
      ['p', `If you have a whole topside and want a change from schnitzel, it is also a good roasting joint. The best piece of beef to roast depends on your budget and taste: topside and [rump](/product/beef-rump-portion/) are lean and economical, while a [standing rib roast](/product/angus-standing-rib-roast-mb3-4/) or [whole eye fillet](/product/whole-beef-fillet/) are the luxury options. Our guide to the [best beef cut for roasting](/blog/best-beef-cut-for-roasting/) compares them all.`],
      ['h2', `Beef schnitzel questions`],
      ['h3', `Why is my schnitzel tough?`],
      ['p', `Either the cut had too much connective tissue, the meat was not pounded thin enough, or it was cooked too long. Use topside or rump, pound to about 5 mm and fry quickly.`],
      ['h3', `Why does the crumb fall off?`],
      ['p', `The meat was too wet, the flour layer was skipped, or the schnitzel was moved too early in the pan. Pat the meat dry, flour it lightly, rest the crumbed pieces before cooking, and only turn them once.`],
      ['h3', `Can I use veal?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Veal makes the classic Wiener schnitzel and is more delicate. The method is identical.`],
      ['h2', `Sauces and sides for schnitzel`],
      ['p', `A great sauce lifts a simple schnitzel into a memorable meal. A creamy mushroom sauce is the pub classic: fry sliced mushrooms in butter with garlic, add a splash of beef stock and some cream, and simmer until thick. A pepper sauce uses the same base with plenty of crushed black peppercorns. For something fresher, spoon over a salsa verde made from parsley, capers, lemon and olive oil, or serve the schnitzel with a squeeze of lemon and a dollop of garlic mayonnaise.`],
      ['p', `For sides, think crisp and fresh to balance the richness of the crumb. A shaved cabbage and apple slaw, a simple tomato and cucumber salad, buttered green beans or a pile of oven chips all work. Mashed potato and peas turn it into a hearty winter dinner, while a big Greek salad makes it lighter for summer.`],
      ['h2', `Getting the oil temperature right`],
      ['p', `If the oil is too cool, the crumb soaks it up and turns greasy before it browns. Too hot, and it burns before the meat is cooked. Test it by dropping in a few breadcrumbs: they should sizzle immediately and turn golden in about 30 seconds. Cook two schnitzels at a time at most, because adding too many at once drops the temperature. Choose a neutral oil with a high smoke point, such as canola, rice bran or sunflower, and top it up between batches if needed.`],
      ['h2', `Planning a slow-cooked meal too?`],
      ['p', `Schnitzel is a quick dinner; for weekends, a slow-cooked beef cheek is the opposite and just as rewarding. You can order [beef cheeks](/product/buy-beef-cheeks/) alongside topside and minute steaks from our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/). Every cut is certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, Australia-wide. For a crumbed chicken version, see [how to crumb chicken](/blog/how-to-crumb-chicken/).`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('satay-beef'),
    title: 'Satay Beef: Grilled Skewers and a Quick Stir-Fry Recipe',
    seoTitle: 'Easy Satay Beef Recipe: Skewers or Stir-Fry',
    excerpt: `Two ways with satay beef: marinated skewers grilled over high heat and a quick stir-fry, with a rich peanut sauce and the best halal cuts for each.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Satay beef is a family favourite in Australia, whether it is marinated skewers straight off the barbecue or a quick stir-fry tossed in creamy peanut sauce. Both versions come from the satay traditions of Indonesia, Malaysia and Singapore, where skewered meat is marinated in turmeric, lemongrass and spices, grilled over charcoal and served with a rich peanut dipping sauce. This guide gives you a beef satay recipe for both styles and explains which cuts to use.`],
      ['h2', `Choosing the beef`],
      ['p', `Satay is cooked quickly over high heat, so it needs a tender cut that stays juicy. Good choices include:`],
      ['ul', [
        `[Rump](/product/rump-beef-steaks/): our top pick for skewers. Flavourful, tender when cut small, and good value.`,
        `[Flank](/product/flank-steak-beef/): coarse-grained and great at absorbing marinade; slice thinly across the grain.`,
        `[Sirloin porterhouse](/product/sirloin-steak-beef-porterhouse/): tender and neat, ideal for a premium stir-fry.`,
        `[Beef stir-fry strips](/product/beef-stir-fry-strips/): ready to cook for a fast weeknight version.`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Is a cheap beef steak OK?`],
      ['p', `Yes, within reason. A cheap beef steak such as rump or flank is ideal for satay, because the marinade, thin slicing and fast cooking all help tenderness. What does not work is a slow-cooking cut such as chuck or blade: those need hours to soften. If you want to use beef flank meat, slice it very thinly across the grain and marinate it for at least two hours.`],
      ['h2', `Beef satay recipe: skewers`],
      ['p', `This beef satay recipe makes about 16 skewers, enough for four as a main.`],
      ['h3', `Marinade`],
      ['ul', [
        `800 g rump or flank, cut into thin strips or 2 cm cubes`,
        `2 stalks lemongrass, white part only, finely chopped`,
        `3 red shallots and 3 cloves garlic`,
        `2 teaspoons ground coriander, 1 teaspoon ground cumin and 1 teaspoon turmeric`,
        `2 tablespoons brown sugar or palm sugar`,
        `2 tablespoons kecap manis or soy sauce, and 2 tablespoons oil`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Blend the marinade ingredients to a paste. Mix with the beef and refrigerate for at least 2 hours, ideally overnight. Soak bamboo skewers in water for 30 minutes so they do not burn. Thread the beef onto the skewers, packing it fairly tightly. Grill over a very hot barbecue or chargrill for 2 to 3 minutes a side, brushing with a little oil, until charred at the edges and just cooked through.`],
      ['h2', `Peanut satay sauce`],
      ['ul', [
        `1 cup roasted unsalted peanuts, finely crushed (or 3/4 cup crunchy peanut butter)`,
        `1 cup coconut milk`,
        `1 to 2 tablespoons red curry paste or sambal oelek`,
        `1 tablespoon each soy sauce and brown sugar`,
        `1 tablespoon lime juice or tamarind`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Fry the curry paste in a little oil for a minute, then add the coconut milk, peanuts, soy and sugar. Simmer for 5 minutes, stirring, until thick. Finish with lime juice and loosen with water if needed. Taste and adjust: it should be rich, sweet, salty and slightly sour.`],
      ['h2', `Satay beef recipe: quick stir-fry`],
      ['p', `For a satay beef recipe that is on the table in 20 minutes, slice 600 g rump or porterhouse into thin strips and toss with a tablespoon of soy sauce and a teaspoon of cornflour. Stir-fry the beef in batches in a very hot wok with a little oil for 1 to 2 minutes until browned; remove. Stir-fry a sliced onion, a red capsicum, broccoli florets and a handful of green beans for 3 minutes. Return the beef, add 3/4 cup of the peanut sauce and toss for a minute until glossy. Serve with jasmine rice or noodles and a scatter of crushed peanuts and coriander.`],
      ['h2', `Tips for the best satay`],
      ['ul', [
        `Marinate long enough: at least 2 hours for skewers.`,
        `Cut the beef small and even so it cooks in minutes.`,
        `Get the grill very hot; satay needs charring at the edges.`,
        `Stir-fry in batches so the beef sears instead of steaming.`,
        `Make the sauce ahead: it keeps for 4 days in the fridge and thickens as it cools.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `What to serve with satay`],
      ['p', `Serve skewers with compressed rice cakes (ketupat or lontong), sliced cucumber and red onion, and the peanut sauce for dipping. A stir-fry pairs with steamed rice or egg noodles. For a party spread, add a [Thai beef salad](/blog/thai-beef-salad/) and some [chicken wings](/blog/chicken-wing-meals/).`],
      ['h2', `Satay beef questions`],
      ['h3', `Can I bake satay skewers?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Cook them under a very hot grill (broiler) for 3 to 4 minutes a side, or bake on a rack at 230°C for 8 to 10 minutes, turning once. You will get less char than on a barbecue but still a great result.`],
      ['h3', `Is satay sauce safe for nut allergies?`],
      ['p', `No. Peanut sauce is made from peanuts. For guests with allergies, serve the skewers with a sweet chilli or soy-ginger dipping sauce instead, and cook them on a clean grill.`],
      ['h3', `Can I make satay with chicken or lamb?`],
      ['p', `Absolutely. Chicken thigh is the most popular satay meat in Malaysia, and lamb backstrap or leg works beautifully too. The marinade and sauce are the same.`],
      ['h2', `Making satay ahead for a party`],
      ['p', `Satay is perfect party food because almost everything can be prepared in advance. Marinate the beef the night before, thread the skewers in the morning and keep them covered on trays in the fridge. Make the peanut sauce a day or two ahead; it thickens in the fridge, so warm it gently with a splash of water before serving.`],
      ['p', `On the day, all that is left is grilling. Cook the skewers in batches and keep them warm on a tray in a low oven while you finish the rest. Serve them on a platter with the warm sauce, rice cakes and cucumber, and let guests help themselves. Allow three to four skewers per person if satay is the main dish, or two each as part of a larger spread.`],
      ['h2', `Kecap manis and other ingredients`],
      ['p', `Kecap manis is a thick, sweet Indonesian soy sauce flavoured with palm sugar. It gives satay its glossy, caramelised finish and is sold at Asian grocers and most supermarkets. If you cannot find it, mix regular soy sauce with brown sugar in equal parts and simmer for a minute until syrupy. Fresh lemongrass and turmeric are also worth seeking out: they give the marinade a bright, fragrant flavour that dried versions cannot quite match. Lemongrass freezes well, so buy a bundle and keep the extra stalks in the freezer.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal beef for satay`],
      ['p', `Rump, flank, porterhouse and stir-fry strips are all in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), along with ready-made [beef kebab skewers](/product/beef-kebab-skewers/). Every product is certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, Australia-wide.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('shaking-beef'),
    title: 'Shaking Beef (Bò Lúc Lắc): A Quick Vietnamese Recipe',
    seoTitle: 'Shaking Beef Recipe: Quick and Tender',
    excerpt: `Shaking beef, the Vietnamese bò lúc lắc: tender cubes of steak seared and tossed in a garlicky glaze, with watercress and a lime-pepper dip.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Shaking beef is one of the most satisfying dishes in Vietnamese home cooking. Cubes of tender steak are marinated briefly, seared in a smoking-hot wok and tossed (or "shaken") with garlic, butter and a glossy soy glaze, then served on a bed of peppery watercress, tomatoes and onions with a sharp lime, salt and pepper dip. The name, bò lúc lắc, refers to the shaking of the pan as the beef cooks. It takes about 20 minutes and tastes like a restaurant dish.`],
      ['h2', `Where shaking beef comes from`],
      ['p', `The dish reflects French influence in Vietnam: cubed steak, butter and watercress meet soy, oyster sauce, garlic and fish sauce. It was once a special-occasion dish because beef was expensive, and it is still a favourite at celebrations and in Vietnamese restaurants around the world, including across Sydney.`],
      ['h2', `The best beef for beef shaking`],
      ['p', `Because the beef is cooked for only a few minutes and served pink in the middle, you need a tender steak. For beef shaking, choose:`],
      ['ul', [
        `[Eye fillet](/product/beef-eye-fillet-steaks/): the classic choice, buttery and very tender.`,
        `[Scotch fillet](/product/scotch-fillet-ribeye-steaks/): juicier and richer, with more marbling.`,
        `[Rump](/product/rump-beef-steaks/): the value option, with plenty of flavour.`,
        `[Sirloin porterhouse](/product/sirloin-steak-beef-porterhouse/): firm, neat cubes that sear well.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Cut the beef into 2.5 to 3 cm cubes, trimming away any sinew. Large cubes let you build a crust while keeping the centre pink.`],
      ['h3', `Why not thin cut beef?`],
      ['p', `Thin cut beef is perfect for stir-fries such as [rainbow beef](/blog/rainbow-beef/) or [beef and black bean](/blog/beef-and-black-bean/), but shaking beef is about thick, juicy cubes with a seared crust. Thin slices overcook before they brown.`],
      ['h2', `Shaking beef recipe`],
      ['p', `This shaking beef recipe serves four with rice.`],
      ['h3', `Marinade`],
      ['ul', [
        `700 g eye fillet, scotch fillet or rump, cut into 3 cm cubes`,
        `2 tablespoons oyster sauce`,
        `1 tablespoon soy sauce and 1 teaspoon fish sauce`,
        `1 tablespoon brown sugar`,
        `4 cloves garlic, minced, and 1 teaspoon cracked black pepper`,
        `1 tablespoon oil`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `To finish`],
      ['ul', [
        `2 tablespoons oil and 2 tablespoons butter`,
        `1 red onion, cut into wedges`,
        `3 cloves garlic, chopped`,
        `2 tablespoons rice vinegar or lime juice`,
        `2 bunches watercress (or rocket), and 2 tomatoes, sliced`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Lime dipping sauce`],
      ['p', `Mix 1 teaspoon salt, 1 teaspoon freshly cracked black pepper and the juice of 2 limes in a small bowl.`],
      ['h3', `Method`],
      ['p', `Mix the beef with the marinade and leave for 30 minutes to 2 hours. Arrange the watercress and tomatoes on a platter. Heat a wok or heavy frying pan until smoking hot and add the oil. Add the beef in a single layer (cook in two batches if needed) and leave it untouched for 1 minute to sear. Shake the pan to turn the cubes, and cook for another 2 to 3 minutes until browned on all sides but still pink in the middle. Add the onion, garlic and butter and toss for 1 minute more. Splash in the vinegar or lime juice, toss, and pour everything over the watercress. Serve immediately with the dipping sauce and steamed rice.`],
      ['h2', `Tips for a great sear`],
      ['ul', [
        `Pat the beef dry after marinating so it browns rather than steams.`,
        `Use the hottest pan you can; a cast iron pan works well if you do not have a wok.`,
        `Do not overcrowd: two batches give far better results than one crowded pan.`,
        `Let the beef sit before shaking so a crust forms.`,
        `Serve straight away, while the watercress just begins to wilt under the hot beef.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Variations`],
      ['p', `Serve shaking beef with tomato rice (rice fried with tomato paste and garlic), or with chips in the French style. For a lighter meal, serve it over a big leafy salad with cucumber and herbs. Some cooks add capsicum chunks with the onion for colour.`],
      ['h2', `Braised cheek beef for slower days`],
      ['p', `Shaking beef is a fast, fiery dish. When you have more time, Vietnamese cooks also love slow-cooked beef: braised cheek beef in a star anise and lemongrass broth becomes so tender it falls apart. Our [beef cheeks](/product/buy-beef-cheeks/) and our guide to [braised beef](/blog/braised-beef/) show you how.`],
      ['h2', `Shaking beef questions`],
      ['h3', `Can I use a cheaper steak?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Rump gives excellent flavour at a lower price. Cut it slightly smaller (2 cm cubes) and do not overcook it, because it is a little firmer than fillet.`],
      ['h3', `Is shaking beef spicy?`],
      ['p', `No, it is savoury and peppery rather than hot. Add sliced fresh chilli to the dipping sauce if you like heat.`],
      ['h3', `What can I use instead of watercress?`],
      ['p', `Rocket, baby spinach or crisp lettuce all work. Watercress is traditional because its pepperiness balances the rich beef.`],
      ['h2', `Making shaking beef for a crowd`],
      ['p', `Shaking beef depends on a very hot pan, so it is best cooked in small batches. For six to eight people, double the marinade and cook the beef in four batches, transferring each one to a warm platter of watercress as it is done. Toss the onions, garlic and butter in the last batch and pour everything, including the pan juices, over the top. Serve it at the centre of the table with bowls of rice and the lime dipping sauce, and let everyone help themselves.`],
      ['p', `If you have a large barbecue with a flat plate, you can also cook shaking beef outdoors. The plate gets very hot and has plenty of room, so you can cook a larger batch at once. Keep the cubes moving with two spatulas, and add the onion, garlic and butter at the end.`],
      ['h2', `What to serve alongside`],
      ['p', `Shaking beef sits beautifully in a Vietnamese-style family meal. Pair it with fresh rice paper rolls, a crunchy cabbage and herb salad dressed with lime and fish sauce, and a simple clear soup. For a heartier meal, serve it with garlic fried rice or crusty bread to soak up the buttery pan juices, as many Vietnamese families do.`],
      ['p', `Leftovers, if there are any, are best eaten the next day tossed through hot rice with a fried egg on top. Reheat the beef very briefly in a hot pan so it does not overcook, and add fresh watercress rather than reheating the old leaves.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal steak for shaking beef`],
      ['p', `Eye fillet, scotch fillet, rump and porterhouse are all in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia. Free delivery applies to orders over $500.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('rainbow-beef'),
    title: 'Rainbow Beef: A Colourful 20-Minute Stir-Fry Recipe',
    seoTitle: 'Quick Rainbow Beef Recipe for Weeknights',
    excerpt: `A rainbow beef stir-fry with tender strips of beef, crunchy capsicum and vegetables in a glossy sauce, plus which cuts to buy and the trick to tender beef.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Rainbow beef is a much-loved Australian takeaway and home-cooking favourite: thin strips of tender beef stir-fried with a rainbow of capsicums, carrot, snow peas and spring onions in a sweet, savoury, glossy sauce. It is fast, colourful and endlessly adaptable, which is why it has become a weeknight staple in so many households. This guide gives you a reliable rainbow beef recipe, explains how to keep the beef tender, and answers the question every curious cook eventually asks: why does sliced beef sometimes show a rainbow sheen?`],
      ['h2', `What is rainbow beef?`],
      ['p', `The dish takes its name from the vegetables: red, yellow and green capsicum, orange carrot, and green snow peas or beans. It is a Chinese-Australian invention rather than a traditional Chinese dish, and the sauce is usually a blend of oyster sauce, soy sauce, hoisin and a little sugar. Some versions add a sprinkle of sesame seeds or a dash of chilli.`],
      ['h2', `Best cuts of beef for rainbow beef`],
      ['p', `The best cuts of beef for a stir-fry are tender, lean and sliced thinly across the grain:`],
      ['ul', [
        `[Rump](/product/rump-beef-steaks/): our first choice for flavour and value.`,
        `[Flank](/product/flank-steak-beef/): coarse-grained and excellent at absorbing sauce.`,
        `[Sirloin porterhouse](/product/sirloin-steak-beef-porterhouse/): tender and easy to slice neatly.`,
        `[Beef stir-fry strips](/product/beef-stir-fry-strips/): ready-sliced, for the fastest dinner.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Our guide to the [best beef for stir fry](/blog/best-beef-for-stir-fry/) compares these in more detail.`],
      ['h2', `The secret to tender stir-fry beef`],
      ['p', `Restaurants use a technique called velveting. Toss each 500 g of sliced beef with 1 tablespoon soy sauce, 1 teaspoon cornflour, 1 teaspoon oil and a pinch of bicarbonate of soda (baking soda) and leave it for 20 to 30 minutes. The bicarb raises the pH on the surface of the meat, which helps it hold moisture, and the cornflour forms a light coating that protects it in the hot wok. Our guide on [how to tenderise beef steak](/blog/how-to-tenderise-beef-steak/) explains more.`],
      ['h2', `Rainbow beef recipe`],
      ['p', `Serves four.`],
      ['ul', [
        `600 g rump, flank or porterhouse, thinly sliced across the grain`,
        `Velveting mix: 1 tablespoon soy sauce, 1 teaspoon cornflour, 1 teaspoon oil, a pinch of bicarb soda`,
        `1 each red, yellow and green capsicum, thinly sliced`,
        `1 carrot, cut into matchsticks`,
        `150 g snow peas, and 4 spring onions cut into 4 cm lengths`,
        `3 cloves garlic and a 3 cm piece of ginger, finely chopped`,
        `Sauce: 3 tablespoons oyster sauce, 2 tablespoons soy sauce, 1 tablespoon hoisin, 1 teaspoon sugar, 1 teaspoon cornflour, 1/3 cup water`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Method`],
      ['p', `Toss the beef with the velveting mix and set aside for 20 minutes. Mix the sauce ingredients in a jug. Heat a wok until smoking, add a tablespoon of oil and stir-fry the beef in two batches for 1 to 2 minutes each, until browned but still slightly pink. Remove to a plate.`],
      ['p', `Add a little more oil, then the garlic and ginger for 20 seconds, followed by the carrot for 1 minute, and the capsicum and snow peas for 2 minutes. Return the beef, pour in the sauce and toss for about 1 minute until glossy and thickened. Add the spring onions, toss and serve with jasmine rice or noodles.`],
      ['h2', `What is the rainbow on beef?`],
      ['p', `If you have ever noticed a shimmering, oil-slick-like rainbow on beef slices, especially on cooked, sliced roast beef or corned silverside, you have seen iridescence. It happens when light hits the cut ends of tightly packed muscle fibres and splits into colours, much like a CD or a soap bubble. It is a physical effect of how the meat was sliced, not a sign of spoilage or added chemicals. It appears most often on meat cut cleanly across the grain. Spoiled meat, by contrast, smells sour and feels slimy. When in doubt, trust your nose and the use-by date.`],
      ['h2', `Variations`],
      ['ul', [
        `Add a teaspoon of chilli bean paste or sliced fresh chilli for heat.`,
        `Swap snow peas for broccoli, bok choy or green beans.`,
        `Toss through cooked hokkien noodles for a one-pan dinner.`,
        `Use chicken thigh or lamb backstrap instead of beef.`,
        `Finish with toasted sesame seeds and a drizzle of sesame oil.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Rainbow beef questions`],
      ['h3', `Can I make it ahead?`],
      ['p', `Slice the vegetables and velvet the beef up to a day ahead and keep them covered in the fridge. The actual stir-fry takes under 10 minutes and is best cooked just before serving.`],
      ['h3', `Why is my stir-fry watery?`],
      ['p', `The wok was not hot enough or was overcrowded, so the beef and vegetables released their juices and stewed. Cook the beef in batches and keep the heat high.`],
      ['h3', `Is rainbow beef suitable for kids?`],
      ['p', `Yes. It is mild, a little sweet and full of colourful vegetables, which makes it a favourite with children. Leave out any chilli and serve it with plain rice.`],
      ['h2', `Try something different: tongue beef`],
      ['p', `If you enjoy exploring the whole animal, tongue beef is a delicacy in many cuisines, from Middle Eastern sandwiches to Japanese grilled gyutan. Simmered until tender, peeled and sliced, it is rich and silky. You will find [beef tongue](/product/beef-tongue/) in our [offal range](/shop/offal-beef-organ-meat/).`],
      ['h2', `Cutting the vegetables for even cooking`],
      ['p', `The key to a stir-fry where every vegetable is crisp-tender at the same moment is cutting them to match their cooking time. Hard vegetables such as carrot need thin matchsticks so they soften quickly. Capsicum can be sliced a little thicker, about 5 mm, because it softens fast. Snow peas cook in a minute and can go in whole. Spring onions go in last, just to wilt. Cutting everything to a similar length, around 5 cm, also makes the dish look neat and makes it easy to eat with chopsticks.`],
      ['p', `Prepare every ingredient before you light the burner. Stir-frying moves quickly, and there is no time to slice a capsicum once the beef is in the wok. Line everything up in bowls in the order it goes in, with the sauce mixed and ready.`],
      ['h2', `Making rainbow beef healthier`],
      ['p', `Rainbow beef is already a balanced meal, but a few small changes lighten it further. Use salt-reduced soy and oyster sauce, double the vegetables and halve the sugar. Serve it with brown rice or cauliflower rice, or over a bed of crisp lettuce leaves. Use a lean cut such as rump or porterhouse trimmed of visible fat, and cook it in a non-stick wok with just a teaspoon or two of oil per batch.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal beef for stir-fry`],
      ['p', `Rump, flank, porterhouse and stir-fry strips are all in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, Australia-wide.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-and-black-bean'),
    title: 'Beef and Black Bean: A Classic Chinese Stir-Fry Recipe',
    seoTitle: 'Beef and Black Bean Recipe: Easy Stir-Fry',
    excerpt: `How to make beef and black bean stir-fry at home: fermented black beans, garlic, capsicum and tender beef in a glossy sauce, ready in about 25 minutes.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Beef and black bean is a Cantonese classic and one of the most ordered dishes on Chinese restaurant menus across Australia. The star is douchi, salted fermented black soybeans, which give the sauce its intense savoury depth. Combined with garlic, ginger, capsicum, onion and tender slices of beef, it makes a quick, satisfying dinner that tastes far better at home than it does from a takeaway container. This guide gives you a beef and black bean recipe, explains the ingredients and covers a few tasty variations.`],
      ['h2', `What are fermented black beans?`],
      ['p', `Fermented black beans are black soybeans that have been salted and fermented until soft, dark and pungent. They are sold in bags or jars at Asian grocers. They are not the same as the black turtle beans used in Mexican cooking. A tablespoon or two adds a deep, salty, almost earthy flavour that makes the sauce unmistakable. Rinse them briefly to remove excess salt, then roughly chop or mash them with garlic.`],
      ['p', `Jarred black bean sauce is a convenient alternative. Check the label, as some brands include alcohol-based cooking wine; choose one without it, or make the sauce from whole beans as below.`],
      ['h2', `Choosing the beef`],
      ['p', `As with any stir-fry, use a tender cut sliced thinly across the grain. [Rump](/product/rump-beef-steaks/) gives great flavour and value, [flank](/product/flank-steak-beef/) soaks up the sauce, and [porterhouse](/product/sirloin-steak-beef-porterhouse/) is tender and neat. Our ready-cut [beef stir-fry strips](/product/beef-stir-fry-strips/) save time on busy nights.`],
      ['h2', `Beef and black bean recipe`],
      ['p', `Serves four with rice.`],
      ['ul', [
        `600 g rump or flank, thinly sliced across the grain`,
        `Marinade: 1 tablespoon soy sauce, 1 teaspoon cornflour, 1 teaspoon oil and a pinch of bicarb soda`,
        `2 tablespoons fermented black beans, rinsed and roughly chopped`,
        `4 cloves garlic and a 3 cm piece of ginger, finely chopped`,
        `1 onion, cut into wedges`,
        `1 red and 1 green capsicum, cut into squares`,
        `1 to 2 fresh chillies, sliced (optional)`,
        `Sauce: 2 tablespoons oyster sauce, 1 tablespoon soy sauce, 1 teaspoon dark soy, 1 teaspoon sugar, 1 teaspoon cornflour, 1/2 cup water or beef stock`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Method`],
      ['p', `Mix the beef with the marinade and leave for 20 minutes. Mash the black beans with the garlic and ginger. Stir the sauce ingredients together. Heat a wok until smoking, add a tablespoon of oil and sear the beef in two batches for about 1 minute each; set aside. Add a little more oil and stir-fry the onion and capsicum for 2 minutes. Push them to the side, add the black bean mixture and chilli, and fry for 30 seconds until fragrant. Return the beef, add the sauce and toss for 1 minute until glossy. Serve straight away with steamed rice.`],
      ['h2', `Black beans with beef: variations`],
      ['p', `Black beans with beef also work beautifully in other forms:`],
      ['ul', [
        `Black bean beef with noodles: toss through cooked egg noodles or flat rice noodles.`,
        `Black bean beef ribs: steam bite-sized [beef short ribs](/product/buy-beef-short-ribs/) with black beans and garlic, dim sum style.`,
        `Mince version: brown [premium beef mince](/product/premium-beef-mince/) with black beans, garlic and chilli for a quick rice topping.`,
        `Add vegetables: broccoli, bok choy, green beans or bamboo shoots.`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Cooking with 1kg beef mince`],
      ['p', `If you have a 1kg beef mince pack in the fridge, split it: brown half with black beans, garlic and chilli for tonight's rice bowls, and freeze the rest for another meal. Black bean mince keeps for three days in the fridge and makes an excellent filling for lettuce cups. Our [Asian beef mince recipes](/blog/asian-beef-mince-recipes/) have more ideas.`],
      ['h2', `Tips for restaurant-style results`],
      ['ul', [
        `Keep the wok very hot and cook the beef in small batches.`,
        `Do not over-marinate: 20 to 30 minutes is plenty for thin slices.`,
        `Rinse the black beans so the dish is not too salty.`,
        `Add the sauce at the end and cook only until glossy.`,
        `Have everything chopped and ready before you start; stir-frying is fast.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Takeaway favourites at home`],
      ['p', `If you have been searching for mongolian beef near me, you can make that at home too. Our [Mongolian beef slow cooker](/blog/mongolian-beef-slow-cooker/) recipe turns chuck into tender, sticky beef with almost no effort, and it pairs nicely with this black bean stir-fry for a home-cooked banquet.`],
      ['h2', `Beef and black bean questions`],
      ['h3', `Are fermented black beans halal?`],
      ['p', `Fermented black beans are simply soybeans, salt and sometimes ginger, so plain varieties are suitable for a halal kitchen. Always read the label, though, as some flavoured products and jarred sauces contain added cooking wine.`],
      ['h3', `How long do fermented black beans keep?`],
      ['p', `Stored in a sealed jar in the fridge, they keep for many months. They are heavily salted, which preserves them.`],
      ['h3', `Can I make this dish less salty?`],
      ['p', `Rinse the beans well, use salt-reduced soy sauce and skip the dark soy. Add a squeeze of lime at the end to balance the savoury flavours.`],
      ['h2', `Choosing a wok and getting it hot`],
      ['p', `A carbon steel wok is the best tool for this dish. It heats quickly, holds heat well and develops a natural non-stick surface over time. If you do not have one, a large, heavy frying pan or cast iron skillet works well; just cook in smaller batches, because flat pans have less hot surface area than a wok. Whatever you use, heat it until a drop of water sizzles and evaporates instantly before adding the oil.`],
      ['p', `Home burners are much less powerful than restaurant wok burners, so the most important thing you can do is avoid overcrowding. Cook the beef in two or three batches, letting each sit for 30 seconds before tossing, so it browns properly. Then return it all to the wok at the end with the sauce.`],
      ['h2', `Serving a home-style Chinese banquet`],
      ['p', `Beef and black bean is at its best as part of a shared meal. Pair it with a simple stir-fried green vegetable such as Chinese broccoli with garlic, a steamed or fried rice, and perhaps a soup. If you want a second meat dish, a sticky soy chicken or a [satay beef](/blog/satay-beef/) stir-fry offers a contrast in flavour. Everything can be prepped in advance, and the stir-fries cooked in quick succession just before serving.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal beef online`],
      ['p', `Rump, flank, stir-fry strips, short ribs and beef mince are all in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, Australia-wide.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-fajitas'),
    title: 'Beef Fajitas: Sizzling Skirt Steak Fajitas at Home',
    seoTitle: 'Easy Beef Fajitas With Sizzling Steak',
    excerpt: `Beef fajitas at home: a lime and cumin marinade, the best halal steak to use instead of skirt, sizzling capsicum and onion, and all the toppings.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Few dinners are as much fun as beef fajitas. A smoking-hot pan of marinated steak, charred capsicum and onions arrives at the table still sizzling, and everyone builds their own warm tortilla with guacamole, salsa and sour cream. Fajitas began on the ranches of the Texas-Mexico border, where workers grilled the thin, tough skirt steak over open fires, and the dish has been a Tex-Mex favourite ever since. This guide covers the marinade, the cut, the cooking and the toppings.`],
      ['h2', `Beef skirt steak fajitas: the traditional cut`],
      ['p', `The original beef skirt steak fajitas used outside skirt, a long, thin, coarse-grained muscle with a deep flavour. Skirt takes marinade beautifully and cooks in minutes. We do not currently list skirt separately, but two cuts in our range work brilliantly:`],
      ['ul', [
        `[Flank steak](/product/flank-steak-beef/): the most popular fajita cut today. Slightly thicker and leaner than skirt, with the same long grain.`,
        `[Rump](/product/rump-beef-steaks/): tender, flavourful and easy to find, a reliable choice for fajitas beef.`,
        `[Chuck tail flap](/product/beef-chuck-tail-flap/): loose-grained and well marbled, very juicy on a hot grill.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Read our [beef skirt](/blog/beef-skirt/) guide for more on skirt and its relatives.`],
      ['h2', `Fajita marinade`],
      ['p', `This marinade is enough for about 800 g of beef.`],
      ['ul', [
        `Juice of 2 limes (and the zest of 1)`,
        `3 tablespoons olive oil`,
        `3 cloves garlic, crushed`,
        `2 teaspoons ground cumin and 1 teaspoon smoked paprika`,
        `1 teaspoon chilli powder (or 1 chipotle in adobo, chopped)`,
        `1 teaspoon dried oregano and 1 teaspoon brown sugar`,
        `1 teaspoon salt and a handful of chopped coriander`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Whisk everything together, pour over the whole steak in a dish or zip-lock bag and marinate in the fridge for 1 to 6 hours. Because of the lime juice, do not marinate overnight, or the surface of the meat can turn mushy.`],
      ['h2', `How to cook beef fajitas`],
      ['p', `Take the steak out of the marinade, wipe off the excess and pat it dry. Heat a cast iron pan, griddle or barbecue until smoking hot. Cook flank for 4 to 5 minutes a side, or rump for 3 to 4 minutes a side, to medium-rare. Rest for 8 minutes.`],
      ['p', `While the beef rests, slice 2 onions and 3 capsicums (a mix of colours) into strips. Add a little oil to the hot pan and cook the vegetables over high heat for 5 to 6 minutes, tossing occasionally, until charred in places but still with some crunch. Season with salt and a pinch of cumin.`],
      ['p', `Slice the beef thinly across the grain, add it to the pan with the vegetables, squeeze over a little lime and bring the sizzling pan straight to the table.`],
      ['h2', `Toppings and sides`],
      ['ul', [
        `Warm flour or corn tortillas (wrap them in foil and heat in the oven)`,
        `Guacamole or sliced avocado`,
        `Fresh tomato salsa (pico de gallo)`,
        `Sour cream or thick yoghurt`,
        `Grated cheese, pickled jalapeños and lime wedges`,
        `Mexican rice and black beans on the side`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `A budget-friendly mince version`],
      ['p', `If you have a beef mince 1kg pack, you can turn it into a crowd-pleasing fajita-style filling. Brown the mince in batches, drain off excess fat, add the fajita spices (without the lime) and a splash of water, and simmer for 5 minutes. Toss through charred onions and capsicum and finish with lime. Our [premium beef mince](/product/premium-beef-mince/) works perfectly. It is a great option when feeding a big group on a budget.`],
      ['h2', `Tips for the best fajitas`],
      ['ul', [
        `Cook the steak whole, then slice it: it stays juicier than pre-sliced strips.`,
        `Get the pan very hot for a good char on the beef and vegetables.`,
        `Always slice across the grain.`,
        `Keep tortillas warm and soft in a clean tea towel.`,
        `Set out toppings in bowls and let everyone build their own.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Beef fajitas questions`],
      ['h3', `What is the difference between fajitas and tacos?`],
      ['p', `Fajitas are grilled strips of meat served with grilled capsicum and onion, usually in soft flour tortillas, and assembled at the table. Tacos use a wide variety of fillings, often in small corn tortillas.`],
      ['h3', `Can I cook fajitas in the oven?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Spread the sliced vegetables on a tray, roast at 220°C for 15 minutes, then add thinly sliced marinated beef and roast for 5 to 8 minutes more. You lose some char but gain convenience.`],
      ['h3', `How much beef per person?`],
      ['p', `Allow about 150 to 200 g of raw steak per person, depending on appetites and how many toppings you serve.`],
      ['h2', `Making guacamole and salsa`],
      ['p', `Homemade toppings take only a few minutes and make the meal. For guacamole, mash two ripe avocados with the juice of a lime, half a finely chopped red onion, a chopped tomato, a little chopped chilli, a handful of coriander and salt. Taste and add more lime or salt until it is bright and well seasoned. Press cling film onto the surface to stop it browning if you make it ahead.`],
      ['p', `For a quick pico de gallo salsa, dice four ripe tomatoes, half a white onion and one or two jalapeños. Mix with chopped coriander, lime juice and salt, and let it stand for 15 minutes so the flavours mingle. A simple sour cream sauce can be made by stirring lime zest, a pinch of cumin and salt into sour cream or thick yoghurt.`],
      ['h2', `Fajitas for a party`],
      ['p', `Fajitas are ideal for entertaining because guests build their own. Marinate the beef in the morning, slice the vegetables ahead, and set up a topping bar before people arrive. Cook the steak and vegetables just before serving and bring them to the table in the sizzling pan. Allow two or three tortillas per person, plus extras for bigger appetites. You can also offer a chicken option: chicken thigh fillets take the same marinade and cook in about the same time.`],
      ['p', `Leftover fajita beef and vegetables make an excellent quesadilla filling the next day: layer them with grated cheese between two tortillas and toast in a dry pan until golden and melted.`],
      ['p', `Leftover fajita beef also keeps well for two days in the fridge and makes a quick lunch bowl with rice, beans, salsa and avocado.`],
      ['h2', `Prefer a crumbed classic?`],
      ['p', `If you were searching beef schnitzel near me rather than Tex-Mex, our [beef schnitzel](/blog/beef-schnitzel/) guide shows how to make crisp, tender schnitzels at home with topside or minute steaks. All the cuts mentioned here are in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-skewers'),
    title: 'Beef Skewers: Marinades, Cuts and Grilling Times',
    seoTitle: 'Beef Skewers: Easy Marinades and Tips',
    excerpt: `How to make juicy beef skewers on the barbecue: the best halal cuts, five easy marinades, how to thread and grill them, and timings for every doneness.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Beef skewers are the easiest way to turn a barbecue into a feast. Cut the beef into even cubes, marinate it, thread it with vegetables, and in under 10 minutes on a hot grill you have juicy, charred skewers that suit everything from a family dinner to a big Eid gathering. This guide covers the best cuts, marinades from around the world, how to thread and grill, and how long to cook them.`],
      ['h2', `Best cuts for beef skewers`],
      ['p', `Because skewers cook quickly over high heat, use a tender cut with a little marbling. Our recommendations:`],
      ['ul', [
        `[Rump](/product/rump-beef-steaks/): the best all-rounder. Flavourful, tender in small cubes and good value.`,
        `[Sirloin porterhouse](/product/sirloin-steak-beef-porterhouse/): tender, neat cubes that hold their shape.`,
        `[Scotch fillet](/product/scotch-fillet-ribeye-steaks/): the juiciest option, thanks to its marbling.`,
        `[Eye fillet](/product/beef-eye-fillet-steaks/): the most tender, best for a special occasion.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `If you would rather skip the prep, our ready-to-cook [beef kebab skewers](/product/beef-kebab-skewers/) go straight onto the grill. Avoid chuck, blade and gravy beef; those beef steaks are excellent for braising but tough when grilled quickly.`],
      ['h2', `How to cut and thread beef on a skewer`],
      ['p', `Trim off any sinew and cut the beef into even 3 cm cubes, so every piece cooks at the same rate. When threading beef on a skewer, leave a tiny gap between the pieces so heat reaches every side; packing them too tightly leaves the centres undercooked. If adding vegetables, use ones that cook in a similar time: capsicum, red onion, zucchini, mushrooms and cherry tomatoes. For best results, thread vegetables on separate skewers, as they need different timing from the meat.`],
      ['p', `Soak bamboo skewers in water for at least 30 minutes so they do not burn, or use flat metal skewers, which also stop the meat spinning when you turn it.`],
      ['h2', `Five marinades for skewers beef lovers will enjoy`],
      ['h3', `1. Middle Eastern`],
      ['p', `Olive oil, lemon juice, garlic, ground cumin, coriander, paprika, allspice and a pinch of cinnamon. Serve with garlic sauce, flatbread and tabbouleh.`],
      ['h3', `2. Greek-style`],
      ['p', `Olive oil, lemon zest and juice, garlic, dried oregano and black pepper. Serve with tzatziki and a Greek salad.`],
      ['h3', `3. Teriyaki-style`],
      ['p', `Soy sauce, brown sugar, grated ginger, garlic and a splash of rice vinegar, simmered until slightly thick. Brush on in the final minutes of grilling so the sugar does not burn.`],
      ['h3', `4. Satay`],
      ['p', `Lemongrass, shallots, turmeric, coriander and kecap manis, served with peanut sauce. See our full [satay beef](/blog/satay-beef/) recipe.`],
      ['h3', `5. Chimichurri`],
      ['p', `Marinate in olive oil, garlic and salt, then spoon fresh chimichurri (parsley, oregano, garlic, chilli, olive oil and lemon juice) over the skewers after grilling.`],
      ['p', `Marinate for 2 to 12 hours in the fridge. Very acidic marinades should be kept to 4 hours or less.`],
      ['h2', `Grilling times`],
      ['p', `Heat the barbecue to high. Oil the grill bars. Cook 3 cm beef cubes for:`],
      ['ul', [
        `Rare: 5 to 6 minutes in total`,
        `Medium-rare: 7 to 8 minutes in total`,
        `Medium: 9 to 10 minutes in total`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Turn the skewers every 2 minutes so all sides char evenly. Rest them for 3 to 5 minutes before serving. If you have an instant-read thermometer, medium-rare is about 55 to 57°C in the centre after resting.`],
      ['h2', `Serving ideas`],
      ['p', `Serve beef skewers on a platter with warm flatbread, hummus, garlic sauce, pickles and a big salad. Or slide the beef off the skewers into wraps with lettuce, tomato and sauce. For a bigger barbecue spread, add [lamb skewers](/blog/lamb-skewers/) and [chicken wings](/blog/chicken-wing-meals/).`],
      ['h2', `Planning a bigger barbecue?`],
      ['p', `For large gatherings, a whole brisket smoked low and slow makes a dramatic centrepiece alongside skewers. The beef brisket price per kg is usually lower than steak, so it is an economical way to feed a crowd. Our [whole brisket](/product/buy-beef-brisket-whole/) and guide to the [best temperature to smoke beef brisket](/blog/best-temperature-to-smoke-beef-brisket/) cover everything you need.`],
      ['h2', `Beef skewers questions`],
      ['h3', `Why are my skewers dry?`],
      ['p', `They were overcooked, the cubes were too small, or the cut was too lean. Use 3 cm cubes of rump or scotch fillet, grill over high heat and take them off at medium-rare to medium.`],
      ['h3', `Can I cook skewers in the oven?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Place them on a rack over a tray and cook under a very hot grill (broiler) for 8 to 10 minutes, turning every few minutes.`],
      ['h3', `Can I prepare skewers ahead?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Thread marinated skewers up to 24 hours ahead and keep them covered in the fridge.`],
      ['h2', `Choosing and using skewers`],
      ['p', `Bamboo skewers are cheap and disposable but need soaking so they do not burn. Metal skewers are reusable and conduct heat into the centre of the meat, helping it cook a little faster and more evenly. Flat metal skewers are the best of all, because the meat does not spin when you turn them. Whichever you use, leave 3 to 4 cm of bare skewer at the end so you have something to hold.`],
      ['p', `For thicker kebabs, you can thread the meat onto two parallel skewers. This makes them much easier to turn and stops the cubes rotating, so every side gets an even char.`],
      ['h2', `Sauces and dips`],
      ['p', `Good dips make skewers a meal. Lebanese garlic sauce (toum) is a classic partner for beef, made by blending garlic, salt and lemon juice with oil until fluffy and white. Tzatziki adds cool freshness, chimichurri brings herbs and acidity, and a simple yoghurt and mint sauce suits almost any marinade. For a spicy kick, serve a harissa yoghurt or a fresh tomato and chilli relish.`],
      ['h2', `Vegetable skewers on the side`],
      ['p', `Thread chunks of capsicum, red onion, zucchini, mushrooms and cherry tomatoes on separate skewers, brush with olive oil, season and grill alongside the beef. They need about the same time as medium-rare beef, but cooking them separately means you can take each off at its best. Halloumi cubes also grill beautifully on skewers.`],
      ['p', `Grilled vegetable skewers also make the meal more welcoming for any vegetarian guests. Cook them on a clean section of the grill, before the meat, and keep them warm on a tray until everything is ready.`],
      ['p', `Leftover beef skewers keep for two days in the fridge. Slide the meat off, slice it and reheat it briefly in a hot pan for wraps or a quick fried rice.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal beef for skewers`],
      ['p', `Rump, porterhouse, scotch fillet, eye fillet and ready-made kebab skewers are all in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('tataki-beef'),
    title: 'Tataki Beef: Seared, Sliced and Served With Ponzu',
    seoTitle: 'Tataki Beef Recipe: Easy Seared Beef',
    excerpt: `How to make tataki beef at home: choosing the cut, searing and chilling, slicing paper-thin and an alcohol-free citrus ponzu dressing, with Wagyu tips.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Tataki is a Japanese technique that makes a small piece of beef go a long way and look spectacular on the plate. A whole piece of tender beef is seared hard on every side, chilled, then sliced paper-thin to reveal a ring of browned crust around a deep pink centre. Dressed with a sharp citrus soy sauce, spring onion and garlic chips, beef tataki is a stunning starter or light meal. It is easier than it looks, and this guide walks you through every step.`],
      ['h2', `What is tataki?`],
      ['p', `In Japanese, tataki means "pounded" or "hit". The technique originally comes from Kochi, where bonito fish was seared over straw fires and then patted with seasonings. Today, tataki beef is common on Japanese restaurant menus worldwide. Because the beef is mostly rare, it is all about quality and freshness: buy a good cut, keep it cold, and handle it with clean equipment.`],
      ['h2', `Best beef cuts for tataki`],
      ['p', `The best beef cuts for tataki are tender, uniform in shape, and lean enough to slice cleanly:`],
      ['ul', [
        `[Eye fillet](/product/beef-eye-fillet-steaks/): the classic. Very tender, with an even, round shape that slices beautifully.`,
        `[Sirloin porterhouse](/product/sirloin-steak-beef-porterhouse/): a little firmer with more flavour.`,
        `[Rump](/product/rump-beef-steaks/): good value, flavourful; choose a thick, even piece.`,
        `[Wagyu eye fillet](/product/wagyu-eye-fillet-steaks-mb5/) or [Wagyu rump](/product/wagyu-beef-rump-steak-mb5/): for the most luxurious result.`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Wagyu beef tataki`],
      ['p', `Wagyu beef tataki is a restaurant favourite because Wagyu's marbling melts slightly during searing and gives each slice a rich, buttery texture. With very high marble scores, slice even thinner and serve smaller portions, as it is very rich. Our [Wagyu steak](/wagyu-steak/) page explains marble scores and cuts.`],
      ['h2', `Tataki beef recipe`],
      ['p', `Serves four as a starter.`],
      ['ul', [
        `400 g eye fillet or porterhouse, in one thick, even piece`,
        `Salt, cracked black pepper and 1 tablespoon oil`,
        `2 spring onions, finely sliced`,
        `1 small piece of ginger, finely grated`,
        `2 cloves garlic, thinly sliced and fried until golden (garlic chips)`,
        `Toasted sesame seeds, and a few shiso or micro herbs`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Alcohol-free ponzu dressing`],
      ['p', `Traditional ponzu often includes mirin; this halal version leaves it out. Mix 3 tablespoons soy sauce, 2 tablespoons fresh lemon or yuzu juice, 1 tablespoon lime juice, 1 tablespoon rice vinegar and 2 teaspoons sugar until the sugar dissolves. Add a small strip of kombu if you have it and let it infuse for 30 minutes.`],
      ['h3', `Method`],
      ['p', `Trim the beef of any sinew and pat it completely dry. Season it well with salt and pepper. Heat a heavy pan until smoking hot, add the oil and sear the beef for 45 to 60 seconds on each side, including the ends, until deeply browned all over. The aim is a crust about 2 to 3 mm deep with the centre still raw.`],
      ['p', `Immediately plunge the beef into a bowl of iced water for 1 minute to stop the cooking, then pat it dry. Wrap it tightly in cling film and refrigerate for at least 1 hour; chilling firms the beef so it slices cleanly. Using your sharpest knife, slice it as thinly as possible (2 to 3 mm). Fan the slices on a chilled plate, spoon over the ponzu, and scatter with spring onion, ginger, garlic chips and sesame seeds.`],
      ['h2', `Food safety for seared beef`],
      ['p', `Searing kills bacteria on the surface of a whole, intact piece of beef, which is where any contamination would normally be. This is why whole-muscle steaks and tataki can be served rare, while minced meat must always be cooked through. Keep the beef refrigerated until you sear it, use a clean board and knife for slicing, and serve tataki within a few hours. Pregnant women, young children, the elderly and anyone with a weakened immune system may prefer to avoid rare meat.`],
      ['h2', `Serving ideas`],
      ['ul', [
        `As a starter with a simple cucumber and wakame salad.`,
        `Over a bowl of sushi rice with pickled ginger.`,
        `On a bed of daikon and carrot ribbons dressed with ponzu.`,
        `With a dab of wasabi and a little grated daikon.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Planning a bigger meal?`],
      ['p', `If you are cooking for a crowd and plan to smoke a brisket as well, a beef brisket purchase online is easy. Our [whole beef brisket](/product/buy-beef-brisket-whole/) is ideal for low-and-slow cooking, while tataki makes a light, elegant starter to balance the meal.`],
      ['h2', `Tataki beef questions`],
      ['h3', `Is tataki raw beef?`],
      ['p', `The outside is cooked and the inside is rare. It is similar to a very rare roast beef rather than a raw dish such as carpaccio.`],
      ['h3', `Can I make tataki ahead?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Sear and chill the beef up to 24 hours ahead. Slice and dress it just before serving.`],
      ['h3', `What if I prefer it more cooked?`],
      ['p', `Sear for 2 minutes a side and rest before chilling. You will get a thicker cooked band and a pink centre, which is still delicious.`],
      ['h2', `Slicing tips for paper-thin tataki`],
      ['p', `The thinner the slices, the more elegant and tender the tataki. Three things help. First, chill the seared beef thoroughly; a firm piece of meat slices far more cleanly than a warm one. Some cooks even place it in the freezer for 15 to 20 minutes before slicing. Second, use your longest, sharpest knife and slice in one smooth stroke rather than sawing back and forth. Third, slice across the grain, so each piece is tender and holds together.`],
      ['p', `Lay the slices slightly overlapping on a chilled plate, in a circle or a straight line, and dress them just before serving so the citrus does not start to "cook" the meat.`],
      ['h2', `Other dressings for seared beef`],
      ['p', `If you want to try something other than ponzu, seared beef pairs well with many flavours. A sesame dressing of tahini or ground sesame, soy, rice vinegar and a little sugar is rich and nutty. A ginger and spring onion oil, made by pouring hot oil over finely chopped ginger, spring onion and salt, is fragrant and simple. For a Thai twist, try a lime, fish sauce and chilli dressing like the one in our [Thai beef salad](/blog/thai-beef-salad/). Each turns the same seared beef into a completely different dish.`],
      ['p', `Whichever dressing you choose, add it at the very last moment and serve the plate straight away.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal beef for tataki`],
      ['p', `Eye fillet, porterhouse, rump and Wagyu steaks are all in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia.`],
    ],
  },
]
